ClankerExplorer is bogging down badly in very large folders, especially now that thumbnail generation exists.

I want a serious performance pass focused on large-directory scalability, with thumbnailing treated as one major contributor but not assumed to be the only problem.

The goal is simple:

A folder with tens of thousands of files should remain responsive.

Do not just patch the thumbnail code. Profile and analyze the whole large-folder path from enumeration through UI rendering, sorting, selection state, metadata, thumbnail scheduling, watcher behavior, and bindings.

### Part 1 — Profile before optimizing

First identify where the time is actually going.

Instrument/profile:

* directory enumeration
* file metadata/stat calls
* sorting
* filtering
* item/view-model construction
* Avalonia binding/layout/rendering
* virtualization behavior
* thumbnail scheduling
* thumbnail decoding
* thumbnail cache lookup
* filesystem watcher activity
* selection-state updates
* extension/type/icon resolution
* inspector-related work
* any per-item converters or property calculations

Test with realistically large folders, including:

* 1,000 items
* 10,000 items
* 50,000+ items where practical

Report the biggest bottlenecks before and after changes.

Do not optimize based only on assumptions.

### Part 2 — Thumbnailing must be viewport-driven

The thumbnail system must never generate thumbnails for an entire directory just because the folder was opened.

Thumbnail work should be driven by what the user can actually see.

Generate thumbnails only for:

* currently visible items
* a small prefetch zone around the visible viewport

Use roughly 1–2 viewport heights above and below as a starting point.

Priority should be:

1. visible items
2. near-visible/preload items
3. nothing else

Do NOT enqueue thousands of thumbnail jobs for off-screen items.

This is critical.

### Part 3 — Cancel/deprioritize stale thumbnail work

If the user scrolls quickly:

* stop caring about items that are now far outside the viewport
* cancel pending work where safe
* otherwise deprioritize/drop stale queued jobs
* immediately prioritize the newly visible area

Do not allow a huge backlog of outdated thumbnail jobs to build up behind fast scrolling.

A user who rapidly scrolls past 5,000 files should not cause 5,000 thumbnails to decode afterward.

### Part 4 — Scroll debounce

Add a very small debounce before expensive thumbnail generation.

For example, something in the ~50–150 ms range may be appropriate.

Intent:

* if the user is rapidly scrolling, avoid doing expensive decode work for content that is only visible for a few milliseconds
* once scrolling settles, populate the current viewport quickly

Do not make normal slow scrolling feel laggy.

### Part 5 — Persistent thumbnail cache

Add a disk-backed thumbnail cache so thumbnails do not need to be regenerated every time a folder is revisited.

The cache key must invalidate automatically when the source changes.

Use something equivalent to:

* normalized/full source path
* file size
* last-modified timestamp
* thumbnail size class/version

Hash this into a stable cache key/file name.

Do NOT use pathname alone.

If a file changes, the old cached thumbnail should naturally stop matching.

### Part 6 — Thumbnail size classes

ClankerExplorer supports variable thumbnail sizes.

Do not cache every arbitrary display size.

Use a small set of canonical thumbnail sizes, for example:

* 128
* 256
* 512

or another sensible set based on the actual UI.

Choose the nearest suitable cached size and scale for display.

Avoid generating a new cache entry every time the user slightly changes thumbnail size.

### Part 7 — Memory cache

Add a bounded in-memory LRU cache for recently displayed thumbnails.

Purpose:

* scrolling away and back should be instant
* switching tabs/folders briefly should not immediately force disk reads
* memory use must remain bounded

Do not hold every thumbnail from every visited folder forever.

### Part 8 — Disk cache limits and eviction

The disk cache may become large, and that is acceptable if it provides real performance value, but it must be bounded.

Implement:

* configurable maximum disk-cache size
* LRU or similarly sensible eviction
* cache cleanup that does not block normal UI interaction
* a future-compatible way to expose “Clear thumbnail cache”

A starting default in the low-single-digit GB range is reasonable, but choose based on implementation realities.

Keep the limit configurable rather than hard-wiring policy into the architecture.

### Part 9 — Worker pool / concurrency control

Thumbnail generation must use bounded concurrency.

Do not start an unbounded Task per item.

Use a worker queue/pool with a small configurable concurrency limit.

Different file types may have very different costs:

* image decode is relatively cheap
* video frame extraction can be expensive
* 3D preview generation can be expensive
* archives may eventually require separate handling

Prevent a set of expensive thumbnails from consuming all CPU, disk bandwidth, or memory and making the file browser unresponsive.

UI responsiveness has higher priority than thumbnail completion.

### Part 10 — Virtualization audit

Verify that the file list/grid itself is truly virtualized.

I want proof that opening a folder with 50,000 items does not instantiate/render 50,000 full visual rows/tiles.

Check:

* actual Avalonia virtualization behavior
* whether custom controls/templates accidentally disable virtualization
* whether measuring/layout forces realization of all items
* whether dynamic thumbnail sizing breaks virtualization
* whether selection/marquee logic enumerates every visual element
* whether converters/bindings force expensive work for every item

Fix any accidental de-virtualization.

This is one of the highest-priority parts of the audit.

### Part 11 — Avoid expensive eager metadata work

Inspect what happens when directory entries are loaded.

Do not eagerly compute expensive metadata for every file if it is not immediately required.

Examples to examine:

* file type descriptions
* icons
* MIME/type probing
* media metadata
* image dimensions
* duration
* archive information
* shell properties
* hash calculations
* expensive extension resolution

Cheap basic filesystem metadata is fine where needed.

Anything expensive should be lazy/on-demand and preferably viewport-driven.

### Part 12 — Enumeration and progressive loading

Analyze whether directory enumeration itself blocks UI responsiveness.

For very large folders:

* enumerate asynchronously where practical
* avoid blocking the UI thread
* consider progressive/batched population if that improves responsiveness
* do not force the user to wait for every item before seeing anything

If progressive insertion creates expensive repeated sorting/layout, design the batching carefully.

The UI should become usable quickly even if background enumeration is still finishing.

### Part 13 — Sorting performance

Large-folder sorting needs explicit attention.

Inspect:

* repeated sorts during enumeration
* expensive comparison functions
* natural-sort implementation cost
* string allocations
* case normalization allocations
* sorting triggered repeatedly by property changes
* thumbnail completion accidentally triggering resorting

Avoid sorting the entire collection repeatedly while thousands of entries arrive.

Batch or defer sorts where appropriate.

### Part 14 — UI-thread audit

Find work currently happening on the Avalonia UI thread that does not belong there.

Especially inspect:

* filesystem access
* decoding
* cache reads/writes
* sorting
* metadata extraction
* directory enumeration
* watcher processing
* icon resolution

Move appropriate work off the UI thread.

Only marshal the minimum state necessary back to the UI.

### Part 15 — Filesystem watcher scaling

Audit the watcher behavior in massive directories.

Make sure large bursts of events do not cause:

* complete directory reloads
* repeated full sorts
* repeated thumbnail invalidation
* duplicate refreshes
* UI-thread storms

Coalesce/debounce watcher events where appropriate.

Prefer incremental updates over rebuilding the entire folder view.

### Part 16 — Allocation and GC pressure

Look for avoidable allocations in hot paths.

Especially:

* per-item strings
* converters
* LINQ in frequently executed code
* repeated path normalization
* thumbnail buffers
* bitmap lifetimes
* event subscriptions
* repeated collection reconstruction

Use profiling evidence.

Do not micro-optimize irrelevant code, but eliminate obvious high-volume allocation problems that cause GC pauses.

### Part 17 — Fast scrolling must remain smooth

Explicitly test this scenario:

1. Open a directory with many thousands of image/video files.
2. Rapidly scroll through it.
3. Stop at a random location.

Expected behavior:

* scrolling stays responsive
* UI does not freeze while thumbnails catch up
* thumbnail jobs from long-passed items do not continue dominating work
* visible thumbnails populate first
* cached thumbnails appear almost immediately
* CPU/memory usage settles rather than continuously climbing

### Part 18 — Cache correctness

Test cache invalidation scenarios:

* file modified
* file replaced with different contents but same name
* file renamed
* file deleted
* thumbnail-size-class changes
* cache-format/version changes

Make cache versioning explicit so future thumbnail format changes can invalidate old entries safely.

### Part 19 — Failure handling

Thumbnail failures should be cheap.

If a corrupt/unsupported file cannot generate a thumbnail:

* fail gracefully
* show the normal file-type icon/fallback
* do not retry continuously while it remains visible
* optionally cache a short-lived or source-version-specific failure state

One bad media file must not create a hot loop.

### Part 20 — Protect responsiveness over completeness

This is the governing rule:

The file browser must stay responsive even if thumbnails are still loading.

It is always acceptable for a thumbnail to appear later.

It is not acceptable for scrolling, selection, navigation, tab switching, or opening a huge folder to freeze because thumbnail work is trying to finish.

### Deliverables

After the work, report:

* what the main bottlenecks were
* profiling evidence for the worst offenders
* what was changed
* whether list/grid virtualization was functioning correctly
* thumbnail scheduling strategy
* cache architecture
* worker/concurrency strategy
* large-folder benchmark results before and after
* remaining known scaling problems
* any architectural issues that should be addressed later

Add regression/performance tests where practical.

Do not rewrite unrelated parts of ClankerExplorer just for cleanliness.

This is a focused scalability pass aimed at making massive directories feel fast and keeping thumbnail mode usable instead of catastrophic.
